{"id": 87, "ts": 1780444281.0446796, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 5, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I see that both Sarah and James have leaned toward Candidate\u202fC, but I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fD is the safer overall choice for a long\u2011distance pilot. While Candidate\u202fC can make quick, correct decisions and has excellent attention skills, the notes also flag serious crew\u2011cooperation concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, being regarded as egocentric, and a lack of willingness to pursue further education. In a safety\u2011critical cockpit, clear, collaborative communication is non\u2011negotiable, and those traits could undermine crew coordination under pressure.\nCandidate\u202fD, on the other hand, demonstrates the core operational strengths we need: he responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates very well, solves problems extremely well, and takes responsibility seriously. These attributes directly support reliability, stress resilience, decision quality, and responsibility\u2014key criteria for long\u2011haul flights. The concerns about moodiness and being a loner are noted, but they are less directly tied to safety performance than the communication deficits shown by Candidate\u202fC, and they can be addressed through crew\u2011resource\u2011training and mentorship.\nGiven the balance of evidence, I maintain my vote for Candidate\u202fD as the best overall fit."}
